<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    The Fellowship suite, run against a real MySQL database.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS

    The default suite runs on SQLite in memory, which is right: it is fast, it
    needs no setup, and that is what makes it worth running on every change.
    What it cannot do is tell the truth about MySQL, and this module has already
    been bitten twice by the difference.

    SQLite accepts a GROUP BY or DISTINCT that carries an ORDER BY on a column it
    neither groups nor selects. MySQL under ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY refuses it
    outright. Three queries here had that shape, every one green on SQLite and
    broken on the first real request.

    SQLite also stores a string longer than its column without complaint. MySQL
    under STRICT_TRANS_TABLES refuses it with error 1406, so a row the SQLite
    suite imports cleanly can fail on a real database. A dynamic filter value
    over 255 characters is the case that found this, and staging now caps it at
    FellowshipFilterValue::VALUE_MAX_LENGTH so the admin is told which column is
    too long while the file is still on screen. That cap is what the SQLite
    suite can check; that nothing else of the same kind is left is what running
    here checks.

    Those three queries are guarded by assertions on the SQL shape, in
    FellowshipAnalyticsGroupingSqlTest and, for the import tally,
    FellowshipImportSmokeTestFileTest. That is the right guard for a suite that
    cannot execute against MySQL, and it is also the limit of one: a shape
    assertion catches a regression in the pattern it already knows, never the
    next incompatibility of a different kind. Executing the same tests where the
    statements really run, under the production sql_mode, is what closes that.

    This file changes nothing about the default suite. phpunit.xml is untouched
    and still runs on SQLite; this is a second, narrower entry point. The exact
    one-time setup and run commands are in the description of the companion
    command, which `php artisan smoke:fellowship-import-integrity` names, and in
    this project's review notes; the short of it is: create an empty
    `zmedix_testing` database, migrate it once, then point `php artisan test` at
    this config file.

    RefreshDatabase migrates and transaction-wraps `zmedix_testing`, so no run
    can reach the development database. The connection takes `strict` from
    config/database.php, which is what puts ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY and
    STRICT_TRANS_TABLES on the session, and is the whole point of running here.

    This does not replace smoke:fellowship-import-integrity. That command drives
    the real import wizard against the development database and leaves its
    records behind to be inspected; this is the ordinary suite, on MySQL.
-->
<phpunit xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="vendor/phpunit/phpunit/phpunit.xsd"
         bootstrap="vendor/autoload.php"
         colors="true"
>
    <testsuites>
        <!-- Deliberately narrower than the default suite: the point is the one
             module whose SQL is engine-sensitive, not a second full run. -->
        <testsuite name="Fellowship">
            <directory>tests/Feature/Fellowship</directory>
        </testsuite>
    </testsuites>
    <source>
        <include>
            <directory>app</directory>
        </include>
    </source>
    <php>
        <!-- Booting a Filament panel per Livewire test leaves the Fellowship
             suites a few megabytes under PHP's 128M default. -->
        <ini name="memory_limit" value="512M"/>
        <env name="APP_ENV" value="testing"/>
        <env name="APP_MAINTENANCE_DRIVER" value="file"/>
        <env name="BCRYPT_ROUNDS" value="4"/>
        <env name="BROADCAST_CONNECTION" value="null"/>
        <env name="CACHE_STORE" value="array"/>

        <!-- The one difference from phpunit.xml that matters: MySQL, and a
             database of its own rather than the development one. Forced, so a
             .env pointing elsewhere cannot leak in, and DB_URL is blanked for
             the same reason since it would override everything below it. -->
        <env name="DB_CONNECTION" value="mysql" force="true"/>
        <env name="DB_DATABASE" value="zmedix_testing" force="true"/>
        <env name="DB_URL" value="" force="true"/>

        <env name="MAIL_MAILER" value="array"/>
        <env name="QUEUE_CONNECTION" value="sync"/>
        <env name="SESSION_DRIVER" value="array"/>
        <env name="PULSE_ENABLED" value="false"/>
        <env name="NIGHTWATCH_ENABLED" value="false"/>
    </php>
</phpunit>
